# brd-reader (Cursor)

Read an already-written BRD (text or file) and extract understanding — do
NOT author a new BRD. Writing a BRD is a business/system-analyst
responsibility, not this rule's job. Once understanding is confirmed, hand
off to `prd-grill` for the implementation checklist.

## Get the BRD

Use whatever text/file the user already gave. If nothing was given, ask for
it (pasted text or a file path) — don't proceed on a bare backlog
description with no actual BRD; if none exists, point to `prd-grill`
directly instead.

## Extract understanding

From the BRD, extract: the core business need (in the analyst's own terms),
process/flow impact (before → after, or explicit no-change), UI impact
(which screens/forms, new vs. existing), data-dictionary impact (new/changed
fields), explicit out-of-scope, and any gaps/ambiguities/contradictions the
BRD leaves unresolved.

## Confirm before handing off

Summarize the above, flag every gap/ambiguity, and get the user to confirm
or fill in the gaps. Carry unresolved gaps forward as open questions rather
than guessing.

## Hand off

Invoke the `prd-grill` skill/rule, passing the BRD (path or text) plus the
confirmed understanding, so its own grill loop only asks genuinely new
implementation-level questions instead of re-deriving what the BRD already
settles.

## On refine

If the user says the BRD was updated, re-read it, diff against the
previously confirmed understanding, and surface only what changed before
re-confirming.
